DNS

Domain name system resolves the ip address for a requested server by name.

there are several types of DNS server used worldwide:
DNS root server - these are responsivle for the top level domains. they are only requested if the name server does not respond. Thus, a root server is a central interface between users and the content on the internet, as it links domain and IP address. The internet corporation for assigned names and numbers(ICANN) coordinates the work of the root name servers. there are 13 such root servers around the globe.
Authoritative name server - these hold authority for a perticular zone. They only answer queries from their area of responsibility, and their information is binding. If an authoratiative name server cannot answer a client's query, the root name server takes over at that point. Based on the country, company, etc., authoritative nameservers provide answers to recursive DNS nameservers, assisting in finding the specific web server(s).
Non-Authoritiative name server - not responsible for a particular DNS zone. instead, they collect information on specific DNS zones themselves, which is done using recursive or iterative DNS querying
Caching DNS server - these cache information from other name servers for a specified period. the authoritative name server determines the duration of this storage.
forwarding server - these forward DNS queries to another DNS server
resolver - these are not authoritative name servers, but perform name resolution locally in the computer or router.

DNS records look as follows:

A - returns an IPv4 address of the requested domain
AAAA - returns an IPv6 address of the requested domain.
MX - Returns the  responsible mail server
NS - returns the DNS servers of the domain
TXT - this record can contain various information. also, SPF and DMARC entries are set to validate mail traffic and protect it from spam.
CNAME - this record acts as an alias for another domain name. If you want the domain www.example.eu to point to the same IP as example.eu, you would create an A record for example.eu and a CNAME record for www.example.eu
PTR - this works the other way around, by converting IP addresses into valid domain names.
SQA -  provides information about the corresponding DNS zone and email address of the administrative contact.

the DNS server Bind9 is used often on linux based distros.

the local config files are usually:
named.conf.local
named.conf.options
named.conf.log

dangerous settings:
allow-query - Defines which hosts are allowed to send requests to the DDNS server
allow-recursion - Defines which hosts are allowed to send recursive requests to the DNS server.
allow-transfer - Defines which hosts are allowed to recieve zone transfers from the DNS server.
zone-statistics - Collects statistical data of zones

Footprinting DNS

one way we can gather information on DNS servers is by using dig. for example:

