SNMP
Simpe Network Management Protocol was created in order to monitor network devices. This can also be used to handle configuration tasks and change settings remotely. SNMP enabled hardware  incudes routers, switches, servers, IoT devices, and many others. SNMP transmits control commands usung agents over UDP port 161. SNMP also uses "traps" over UDP port 162. These are data packets sent from the SNMP server to the client without being explicitly requested.If a device is configured accordingly, an SNMP trap is sent to the client once a specific event occurs on the server-side. A prerequisite for SNMP functionality is unique identifiers for each device that is known by each device.

MIB(sub-heading)
To ensure that SNMP access works across manufacturers and with different client-server combinations, the Management Information Base was created. MIB is an independent format for storing device information. It is a text file in which all queryable SNMP objects of a device are listed in a standardized tree hierarchy. It contains at least one Object Identifier (OID), which in additin to the necessary unique address and a name, also provides information about the type, access rights, and a descriotion of the respective object. MIB files are written in the Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) based ASCII text format. The MIB's do not contain data, but they explain where to find which information and what it looks like, wich returns values for the specific OID, or which data type is used.

OID(sub-heading)
An OID represents a node in a hierarchical namespace. A sequence of numbers uniquely identifies each node, allowing the node's position in the tree to be determined. The longer the chain, the more specific the information. Many nodes in the OID tree contain nothing except references to those below them. The OIDs consist of integers and are usually concatenated by dot notation. We can look up many MIBs for the associated OIDs in the Object Identifier Registry.

SNMPv1(Sub-Heading)
SNMPv1 is the first version of the protocol and is still used in many small networks. It supports the retrieval of information from network devices, allows for the configuration of these devices, and provides traps, which are notifications of events. However, it has no in built authentication mechanism, meaning any device accessing the network can read and modify network data. Another main flaw of SNMPv1 is that it doesnt support encryption.

SNMPv2(Sub-heading)
SNMPv2 existed in different versions. The version that still exists today is v2c, and the extention c means community based snmp. Reguarding security, SNMPv2 is on par with SNMPv1 and has been extended with additional functions from the party-based SNMP no longer in use. However, a significant problem with the initial execution of the SNMP protocol is that the community string that provides security is only transmitted in plain text, meaning it has not built in encryption

SNMPv3(Sub-heading)
The security has been increase enormously for SNMPv3 by security features like authentication, using username and password and transmissions encryption(via pre-shared key) of the data. However, the complexity also increases to the same extent, with sinificantly more configuration options than vc2.

Community strings
Community strings can be seen as passwords that are used to determine whether the requested infrmation can be viewed or not. It is important to note that many organizations are still using SNMPv2, as the ransition to SNMPv3 can be very complex, but the services still need to remain active. This cuases many administrators a great deal of concern and creates some problems they are keen to avoid. The lack of knowledge about how the information can be obtained and how we as attackers use it makes the administrators approach seem inexplicable. At the same time, the lack of encryption of the data sent is also a problem. Because every time the community strings are sent over the network, they can be intercepted and read


