Windows Remote management protocols

remote management is enabled by default from windows server 2016 onwards.
the three main services for windows remote management are:
Remote Desktop Protodol (RDP)

Windows Remote Management (WinRM)

Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI)


RDP
This protocol allows display and control commands to be transmitted via the GUI encrypted over IP networks.
RDP works at the applocation layer of the TCP/IP model, using port 3389. to connect across two different networks, port forwarding needs to be set up on the hosting router, and the RDP connection must be allowed, as most routers disallow these conenctions by default, for obvious reasons. A workaround that companies often use is a VPN, as this bypasses the need to enable this dangerous setting on their router, as well as not needing to set up port forwarding.

by default, RDP uses TLS/SSL, but in some cases, less secure encryption methods are allowed via RDP security. Despite this use of proper encryption, the certificates are still self signed, meaning an attacker isnt truly blocked from accessing a network because of this security feature. The remote desktop service is installed by default on windows servers and does not require additional external applications. The service can be activated using the server manager, and comes with the default setting to allow connections to the service only to hosts with Network level authentication (NLA)

Footprinting
We can simply scan the default RDP port with the handle: --script rdp* in order to scan with all rdp scripts. This will reveal things like the hostname, the version, and other potentially useful information. However, in an actual deployment, we can see that the RDP cookies(mstshash=nmap) used by nmap to interact with the RDP server can be identified easily, which will likely lock us out on hardened networks

To check RDP settings without authenticating to the server, we can use the tool rdp-sec-check.pl (Look in Tools folder), with the syntax:
./rdp-sec-check.pl {ip_address}

to initiate an RDP session, we can use the syntax:
xfreerdp /u:{username} /p:"{password}" /v:{ip_address}




WinRM
The Windows Remote Management(WinRM) is a simple windows command line remote management protocol. it relies on ports 5985(HTTP) and 5986(HTTPS) for communication. a component of WinRM is Windows Remote Shell (WinRS), which lets us execute commands on the remote system. Services like remote sessions using PowerShell and event log merging require WinRM. It is enabled by default starting with Windows Server 2012, but it must be configured for older server versions and clients, with the necessary firewall exceptions created.

Footprinting
We can often observe that we will only see port 5985(HTTP) being used instead of 5986(HTTPS)

to scan with nmap, we would just use -sC and -sV on the correct ports.

to connect, or test if a connection is possible to a target, we will use evil-winrm, with the syntax:

evil-winrm -i {ip_address} -u {username} -p {password}


WMI
WMI allows read and write access to almost all settings on windows systems.

Footprinting
The initialisation of WMI communication always takes place on TCP port 135, and after the successful establishment of the connection, the communication is moved to a random port.

in order to gain a foothold via WMI, we would use Impackets wmiexec.py with the following syntax:

wmiexec.py {domain}/{username}:"{password}"@{ip_address}





















